+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This FMEA covers the customer, staff and payment journeys implemented by `accounts-service`, including its scheme,
+fraud, ledger and notification integrations. It is based on the code, `README.md`, and the OKF documents in `okf/`.
+The scores describe the current release as reviewed on 4 November 2026; they are not residual risk after the actions
+below are implemented.
+
+Scores are on a 1–10 scale: severity (S) is customer, financial, regulatory or operational impact; occurrence (O) is
+the estimated likelihood under normal operation; detection (D) is how likely the failure is to escape existing checks
+and be found promptly (10 = unlikely to be detected). `RPN = S × O × D`. The highest RPNs should be treated as release
+gates. Occurrence and detection are directional ratings because production incident rates and monitoring definitions
+were not supplied.
+
+## FMEA
+
+| ID | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers | Effect on dependent systems and teams | Detection / existing controls | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| FM-01 | A payment is sent without fraud screening | `FraudScreeningClient` exists, but `PaymentService`/`PaymentDispatcher` never calls it. This violates policy FRD-7. | A fraudulent or sanctioned payment can be sent; a legitimate payment is not held for review when it should be. | Fraud team has no held-payment work; compliance and the bank may face loss and regulatory action. | Code review and fraud-team reconciliation can reveal the gap, but there is no runtime control or metric proving every payment was screened. | 10 | 8 | 9 | **720** | Make screening a mandatory pre-send state transition. Fail closed on `REFER`, timeout and error; persist the decision and correlation ID; add contract and integration tests plus an alert for any sent payment without an `ACCEPT`. **Release blocker.** |
+| FM-02 | Scheme timeout leaves a payment locally absent even though it was accepted | A 10-second scheme timeout may occur after acceptance. `SchemeTimeoutException` is not caught; the transaction rolls back, while the scheme reference may already be live. | Customer sees an error and may retry; the payee can receive money once or twice, with no reliable local status. | 07:00 reconciliation cannot match the scheme settlement to a payment; operations and complaints must investigate manually. | Scheme settlement report and customer complaint may expose an unmatched instruction; there is no status-query or timeout-recovery path. | 10 | 5 | 8 | **400** | Catch timeout, retain the payment and immutable scheme reference, mark `SUBMITTED`/`UNKNOWN`, query `GET /v2/instructions/{reference}`, and reconcile before retrying. Alert on unknown instructions and require idempotent resend of the same reference. **Release blocker.** |
+| FM-03 | Ledger is booked but payment is treated as posting-failed, or recovery debits twice | A ledger timeout can happen after booking. The dispatcher marks `POSTING_FAILED`; a future retry could generate a new UUID instead of reusing `ledgerReference`. | Customer can be debited twice or see a sent payment that appears unresolved. | Core ledger and scheme settlement diverge; finance reconciliation breaks and operations must repost from exceptions. | `POSTING_FAILED` is intended for the daily exceptions report, but no automatic reference-preserving retry or booking query is shown. | 10 | 5 | 7 | **350** | Persist `ledgerReference` before posting, query/retry using that same reference, make recovery an idempotent workflow, and alert on `POSTING_FAILED` age and count. Test timeout-before-booking and timeout-after-booking cases. **Release blocker.** |
+| FM-04 | Two payments pass balance or daily-limit checks and overdraw the account | Checks use the service's copied balance and an unconstrained `paidToday` read; concurrent requests have no locking or reservation. | Payment(s) can exceed available funds or the daily limit; later payments can be rejected unexpectedly when the ledger refreshes the balance. | Core ledger rejects or requires repair; finance sees unexplained balance differences and support receives complaints. | Nightly ledger overwrite and reconciliation eventually reveal the difference, but not at request time. | 9 | 6 | 7 | **378** | Enforce atomic funds/limit reservation (database lock or ledger-authoritative authorization), include queued payments in a durable reservation model, and load-test concurrent requests. Never use the copy for an exact zero-balance decision. |
+| FM-05 | A queued payment is dispatched on a bank holiday | `WorkingDays` excludes only Saturday/Sunday; bank holidays are not represented. The scheme then rejects the instruction with `DT01`. | A payment promised for the next working day is rejected or delayed; the customer may not understand the status. | Scheme rejects a valid customer instruction; operations, support and complaints handle avoidable exceptions. | `REJECTED`/`DT01` and the past-date queue report can identify it after the fact; there is no holiday calendar control. | 7 | 6 | 6 | **252** | Use a versioned UK bank-holiday calendar shared with the scheme, calculate execution dates from it, test year boundaries and holidays, and alert on `DT01` for service-generated dates. |
+| FM-06 | Due queued payments are not sent, or one failed item prevents the rest | Scheduler only runs at 06:00 Monday–Friday; there is no visible job health/claiming mechanism. An exception from `dispatcher.dispatch` can interrupt the loop. | Payment remains `QUEUED` after its promised date; customers experience a silent delay. | Operations' overdue queue grows; scheme volume and finance settlement are incomplete; fraud review timing can be missed. | `QUEUED` payments past execution date are listed for operations, but detection is batch/manual and the job has no success watermark or per-item isolation. | 8 | 5 | 7 | **280** | Make dispatch durable and restart-safe: claim each item, isolate failures, retry with backoff, and record attempts. Add metrics/alerts for job start, completion, duration, overdue queue age and per-item failures; run a controlled replay. |
+| FM-07 | Customer balance or account status is stale after a ledger outage/refresh failure | The balance is a copy overwritten at 02:00; the refresh process is external to this service and no freshness/availability control is shown. | App shows an incorrect balance or permits/rejects a payment using stale information. | Support, finance and closure operations act on inconsistent data; regulatory/customer statements can be wrong. | Reconciliation and the next ledger overwrite can expose drift; clients are not given a balance freshness indicator. | 8 | 6 | 7 | **336** | Monitor ledger refresh freshness and row counts, fail or degrade safely when the copy is stale, expose freshness internally, and make ledger-authoritative checks mandatory for closure and other exact-balance actions. |
+| FM-08 | Notification is missing or misleading after a payment state change | Notifications are best effort and failures are caught/logged with no retry or durable outbox. | Customer may not know a payment was sent, queued, held or failed and may submit a duplicate or contact support. | Notification team has no guaranteed delivery record; support and complaints lack a customer communication audit trail. | Application logs and payment status are available, but there is no delivery status, retry queue or alert threshold. | 5 | 6 | 5 | **150** | Use an outbox keyed by payment and event type, retry safely, record delivery status, and alert on age/backlog. Keep the existing rule that notification failure must never undo a payment. |
+| FM-09 | Cross-tenant or unauthorised data is exposed | Tenant and resource checks are spread across token claims and `AccessPolicy`; a bad identity claim, future endpoint, or missing method check could bypass them. | A customer or staff member sees another bank's account, customer, payment or internal information. | Breach of tenant isolation, data-protection incident, loss of trust and incident response for every affected bank. | API tests cover representative 401/403/404 behaviour, but there is no production authorization audit, deny metric or systematic endpoint matrix. | 10 | 3 | 7 | **210** | Centralise and test authorization invariants: tenant must be bound to trusted identity claims, every resource query must scope tenant, and staff/customer projections must be separate. Add negative contract tests for every endpoint and audit denied/allowed access without logging sensitive data. |
+| FM-10 | Sensitive payment data is returned or logged beyond its intended audience | `PaymentView` returns full payee sort code/account number; payment entities also hold full account details. The notification path correctly uses only the last four digits, but that rule is not a general response/logging policy. | Personal/payment data can be disclosed through API consumers, logs, traces or support exports; customers may suffer fraud or a reportable breach. | Data-protection, security and complaints teams must investigate; downstream channels may retain over-broad data. | Code inspection can find the response shape; there is no redaction test, DLP alert or inventory of downstream retention. | 9 | 4 | 7 | **252** | Define field-level data classification, return masked account details by default, prohibit sensitive fields in logs/traces, add serialization and notification redaction tests, and review existing consumers before changing the contract. |
+| FM-11 | Account closure proceeds without proving a zero ledger balance | The required approver/closure workflow is incomplete in the visible API, and the request path checks only account status; the OKF requires a ledger-authoritative zero-balance check. | Funds can be stranded in a closed account and the customer may lose access to them. | Core ledger, finance and operations need manual remediation; complaints and regulatory handling are impacted. | Pending closure records exist, but there is no shown ledger check, approval action, or alert for closure-with-balance. | 9 | 4 | 8 | **288** | Implement the two-person approval endpoint/workflow, call the ledger for an authoritative balance immediately before approval, reject non-zero/pending payments, and audit the decision and ledger response. |
+| FM-12 | Statements or the regulatory return are missing or incorrect | Statements are a business dependency, but this repository exposes only persistence and no cut/refresh/reporting workflow; stale or wrong ledger balances can be used. | Customer cannot obtain a correct statement and may receive incorrect financial information. | Regulatory return due by the fifth working day can be wrong; finance, complaints and compliance must remediate. | Monthly reconciliation/manual reporting may detect it, but there is no service-level statement completeness or correctness alert. | 8 | 4 | 8 | **256** | Define ownership and an automated monthly close: ledger snapshot, statement completeness/reconciliation checks, immutable period records, and alerts before the fifth working day. Add tests for missing, duplicate and corrected periods. |
+| FM-13 | Time-dependent behaviour occurs on the wrong date/time | Payment cut-off uses the injected UK clock, but `ClosureRequest` and `SupportCase` use `Instant.now()` directly; holiday logic is incomplete and scheduler uses wall-clock execution. | Payments can be queued/sent at the wrong time; audit ordering and customer-visible timelines can be wrong. | Reconciliation, complaints and audit evidence become difficult to trust; tests may pass while production differs by timezone or DST. | Some payment tests use `MutableClock`; there is no clock injection for all domain timestamps or scheduler misfire monitoring. | 6 | 4 | 6 | **144** | Inject one UK clock everywhere, use explicit UTC/UK timestamp policy, test DST/cut-off/holiday boundaries, and monitor scheduler misfires and clock skew. |
+| FM-14 | Integration or unexpected application failure returns an unclassified 5xx and loses the intended state transition | Scheme timeouts and other `RestClient` failures are not consistently mapped; exception handling mainly covers expected business exceptions. A transaction rollback can erase local state after an external side effect. | Customer receives an opaque error and may retry, creating duplicates or support cases. | Channel teams see elevated errors; operations cannot distinguish safe retry from unknown external outcome. | HTTP error rate and logs can detect failures, but no error taxonomy/correlation or safe-retry signal is exposed. | 8 | 5 | 6 | **240** | Define an integration error taxonomy and correlation IDs, persist an explicit `UNKNOWN`/reconciliation state before returning, map errors consistently, and publish retry guidance to channel clients. |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+The following must be addressed or explicitly risk-accepted before release: FM-01 (fraud screening), FM-02 (scheme
+timeout recovery), FM-03 (ledger idempotency), and FM-04 (concurrent funds control). FM-07, FM-11 and FM-12 need an
+operational owner and monitored control even if their implementation is outside this repository. FM-05, FM-06 and FM-08
+should be included in the release runbook with dashboards and replay procedures.
+
+## Verification plan
+
+At minimum, the release evidence should include:
+
+- API and integration tests for fraud `ACCEPT`/`REFER`/`REJECT` and timeout/error fail-closed behaviour.
+- Scheme timeout tests for acceptance-before-timeout and no-acceptance, proving the same scheme reference is reconciled
+  and never duplicated.
+- Ledger timeout tests before and after booking, proving the same `ledgerReference` is used for recovery.
+- Concurrent payment tests around balance and daily limit, queue replay tests, and a bank-holiday calendar test.
+- Negative authorization tests across both tenants and all customer/support/admin/approver projections.
+- A production runbook for overdue queues, unknown scheme instructions, `POSTING_FAILED`, ledger refresh staleness,
+  notification backlog, and statement/closure reconciliation.
